## 1.  Why `std::optional` and `std::variant` are useful

|  | `std::optional<T>` | `std::variant<T…>` |
|--|--------------------|--------------------|
| **Purpose** | “maybe‑present” value (has‑value / null‑opt) | “sum type” (exactly one of several types) |
| **Null state** | `std::nullopt` | No built‑in null state (use `std::monostate` if you need one) |
| **Typical use‑case** | Function return that may fail, field that is optional, “optional” pointer | Polymorphic data that can be one of several concrete types, visitor pattern, discriminated unions, `std::expected` in C++23 |

Because these types are part of the standard library, they are widely accepted, but they are also a *source of subtle bugs* if you treat them like raw pointers or like plain unions.

Below is a curated list of **common mistakes** that show up in real projects, along with concrete ways to avoid them.

---

## 2.  `std::optional` – common pitfalls

| # | Mistake | Why it’s wrong | How to avoid |
|---|----------|----------------|--------------|
| 1 | **Calling `value()` or dereferencing `operator*` without checking `has_value()`** | If the optional is empty, `value()` throws `std::bad_optional_access` (or UB for `operator*`). It can silently mask logic errors. | **Always check** `opt.has_value()` (or use the `bool` conversion) before accessing. <br>Prefer `opt.value_or(default)` or `opt.value_or_throw` (C++23). |
| 2 | **Using the optional as a container for trivial types** (e.g. `optional<int>` to represent “missing int”) | `std::optional<int>` occupies *sizeof(int) + 1 bit* (often 5‑bytes). For many small values, this extra storage is wasteful. | Use `int` with a sentinel value (e.g. `-1` or `std::numeric_limits<int>::max()`) when performance matters. Use `optional` only when the “empty” case is semantically distinct from any valid value. |
| 3 | **Not handling the case of a non‑trivial destructor** | Destructing an optional that contains a type that throws on destruction can trigger `std::terminate`. | Make sure the alternative’s destructor is `noexcept` or use a wrapper type that guarantees `noexcept`. |
| 4 | **Copying / moving an optional that holds a non‑copyable type** | The compiler will delete copy/move operations, often leading to subtle “unused parameter” or “no matching function” errors. | *Explicitly* `std::move` or `emplace` the optional. Use `std::optional<std::unique_ptr<T>>` for move‑only types. |
| 5 | **Implicit conversions that drop the “maybe” semantics** | `optional<T> opt = T{}` creates a *valid* value, but `optional<T> opt = std::nullopt;` creates an empty optional. If you write `auto opt = get_value_or_null();` and forget that `opt` might be null, you’ll hit #1. | Be explicit: use `std::nullopt`, `opt.emplace(...)`, or `opt = std::nullopt;`. |
| 6 | **Using optional with reference types** | `std::optional<T&>` is ill‑formed. The standard forbids reference‑holding optionals because the reference must stay valid. | Store a `std::reference_wrapper<T>` or just use a raw reference if the lifetime is guaranteed. |
| 7 | **Returning `optional` from a function that can legitimately return a “null” value** | If you return `std::optional<Error>` from a function that may *succeed* without error, you might think “no error” means “no value”, but the caller may misinterpret it. | Prefer `std::expected<T, Error>` (C++23) or a pair/struct that contains a `bool success;` flag and the value. |
| 8 | **Default‑constructing an optional with a value type that has a default constructor** | `std::optional<Foo> opt;` will *not* contain a `Foo` unless you call `opt.emplace()`. If you later do `opt = Foo{};`, you may be unintentionally overwriting the value. | Remember that default‑constructing an optional creates an *empty* optional, regardless of the underlying type. |

---

## 3.  `std::variant` – common pitfalls

| # | Mistake | Why it’s wrong | How to avoid |
|---|----------|----------------|--------------|
| 1 | **Accessing the wrong alternative with `std::get<T>`** | If `T` is not the active type, `std::get<T>` throws `std::bad_variant_access`. | Use `std::holds_alternative<T>(v)` or `std::get_if<T>(&v)` to safely test or obtain a pointer. |
| 2 | **Not covering every alternative in a visitor** | `std::visit` requires that the visitor can handle *every* alternative; otherwise you get a hard‑to‑diagnose compile‑error. | Use an *overloaded lambda* that covers all types, or add a fallback `auto&` that uses `static_assert(false, "Unhandled type");`. |
| 3 | **Assuming a default state exists** | Unlike `optional`, `variant` always holds a value (the first alternative is default‑constructed). There is no “empty” variant. | If you need a “null” state, add `std::monostate` as one of the alternatives (`variant<std::monostate, int, std::string>`) and use `std::holds_alternative<std::monostate>(v)` to check. |
| 4 | **Using `variant` for recursive data structures without indirection** | `variant<int, MyVariant>` is ill‑formed because it would be infinite in size. | Use `std::variant<int, std::unique_ptr<MyVariant>>` or `std::shared_ptr<MyVariant>`. |
| 5 | **Copying a variant when one alternative’s copy constructor throws** | The copy/move constructor/assignment of the variant is *conditionally* `noexcept`. If any alternative’s operation throws, the whole variant throws. | Make sure all alternatives are `noexcept` or use `try/catch` around the copy/assignment. |
| 6 | **Misusing `in_place_index` / `in_place_type`** | `v.emplace<0>(value)` can be confusing; if the index is wrong, you’ll silently construct a different alternative. | Prefer `v.emplace<std::string>(std::move(str))` or `v.emplace<0>(42)` only when the meaning of index is crystal clear. |
| 7 | **Assuming a variant can hold reference types** | `variant<T&>` is ill‑formed for the same reason optional cannot hold references. | Use `variant<std::reference_wrapper<T>>`. |
| 8 | **Using variant as a “catch‑all” type (`variant<int, std::string, double> v = something;`)** | Implicit conversions may be ambiguous: `v = 5;` can be `int` or `double`. The compiler will refuse. | Explicitly specify the type: `v = std::in_place_type<int>(5);`. |
| 9 | **Using a variant that contains a large or expensive type in a high‑frequency context** | The storage is the *max size* of all alternatives; copying the variant copies the whole storage even if only a small alternative is active. | Keep alternatives small, or use `std::variant<std::monostate, std::shared_ptr<Foo>>` when copying large objects is expensive. |
| 10 | **Mixing `variant` with `any` or polymorphic hierarchies** | `variant` is *type‑safe* but not a polymorphic base; using it as a stand‑in for virtual inheritance leads to object slicing. | Prefer classic polymorphism (`unique_ptr<Base>`) or `std::any` if you truly need dynamic type erasure. |

---

## 4.  Best‑Practice Checklist

| Category | Rule | Tool / Feature that helps |
|----------|------|---------------------------|
| **Initialization** | `std::optional<T> opt;` → empty. `opt.emplace(args)` → set. | `opt.has_value()` before use. |
| **Safety** | Use `value_or(default)`, `value_or_throw`, `get_if`. | `std::optional<T>::value_or` (C++17) or `std::variant::holds_alternative`. |
| **Exceptions** | Know the exception guarantees of `optional`/`variant` constructors. | `noexcept` specifiers; check `constexpr` `noexcept` of each alternative. |
| **Performance** | Avoid large variants; keep alternatives small. | `static_assert(sizeof(variant) == ...);` |
| **Lifetime** | Do not store references directly in optional/variant. | Use `std::reference_wrapper`. |
| **Null State** | If you need a null/empty state, use `std::monostate` for variant. | `variant<std::monostate, ...>` |
| **Visitor** | Write a single visitor that handles all alternatives. | Overloaded lambda trick: `auto visitor = overloaded{ ... };` |
| **Copy/Move** | Remember that copying a variant copies the *storage*, not just the active alternative. | `std::is_nothrow_move_constructible_v<T>` to reason about safety. |
| **Return Types** | Prefer `std::expected` for “either value or error” (C++23). | `std::expected<T, Error>` |
| **Documentation** | Document the meaning of each alternative and the “empty” state. | Comments or a small enum that maps to the index. |
| **Testing** | Unit‑test every alternative path, especially `value_or` and visitor. | Catch2 or GoogleTest for coverage. |

---

## 5.  Code‑snippets that illustrate the advice

### 5.1.  Safe `optional`

```cpp
std::optional<int> find_index(const std::vector<int>& v, int target) {
    for (size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); ++i)
        if (v[i] == target)
            return static_cast<int>(i);   // explicit cast to avoid ambiguous overload
    return std::nullopt;                 // clear “empty” state
}

auto idx = find_index(nums, 42);
if (idx) {            // bool conversion
    std::cout << "found at " << *idx << '\n';
} else {
    std::cout << "not found\n";
}
```

### 5.2.  Avoiding the “empty” pitfall

```cpp
// Bad: using optional<int> to mean “maybe” int
std::optional<int> opt = -1;   // some sentinel value is ambiguous

// Good: use a separate flag or a monostate
std::variant<std::monostate, int> v = std::monostate{};
if (auto p = std::get_if<int>(&v))
    std::cout << "value: " << *p;
```

### 5.3.  `variant` visitor

```cpp
using MyVariant = std::variant<int, std::string, std::monostate>;

auto visitor = overloaded{
    [](int x)           { std::cout << "int: " << x << '\n'; },
    [](const std::string& s) { std::cout << "string: " << s << '\n'; },
    [](std::monostate)  { std::cout << "none\n"; }
};

MyVariant v = 10;
std::visit(visitor, v);
```

*The `overloaded` helper:*

```cpp
template<class... Fs>
struct overloaded : Fs... { using Fs::operator()...; };
template<class... Fs>
overloaded(Fs...)->overloaded<Fs...>;
```

### 5.4.  Using `monostate` for “null”

```cpp
std::variant<std::monostate, int, std::string> val;

// Initialize to “null”
val = std::monostate{};

if (std::holds_alternative<std::monostate>(val)) {
    std::cout << "no value\n";
} else if (auto* p = std::get_if<int>(&val)) {
    std::cout << "int: " << *p << '\n';
} else if (auto* p = std::get_if<std::string>(&val)) {
    std::cout << "string: " << *p << '\n';
}
```

### 5.5.  Avoiding recursion pitfalls

```cpp
// BAD: will never compile (infinite type)
using Bad = std::variant<int, Bad>;  // ill‑formed

// GOOD: use indirection
using Good = std::variant<int, std::unique_ptr<Good>>;
```

---

## 6.  Take‑away

1. **Never dereference an empty `optional`.**  
   Use `has_value()`, `value_or`, or `value_or_throw` (C++23).

2. **`variant` is not a “null‑optional” type.**  
   Add `std::monostate` if you need a “none” value, or use `std::optional<variant<…>>` if you want both.

3. **Always handle every alternative in a visitor.**  
   The compiler will force you, but being explicit keeps code clear.

4. **Mind the lifetime and copy/move semantics.**  
   Optional and variant do *not* hide the costs of copying the underlying object.

5. **Choose the right tool for the job.**  
   `std::optional` for “maybe present”, `std::variant` for “exactly one of N types”, `std::expected` for “value or error” (C++23), and classic pointers for polymorphic hierarchies.

By keeping these rules in mind—and by writing unit tests that cover the “empty” and “wrong alternative” paths—you can use `std::optional` and `std::variant` safely and efficiently in any C++ codebase.
